  "evidences": [
    {
      "id": "ev_1",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s1"
      },
      "text": "securelist.com /olympic-destroyer-is-still-alive/86169/ We have previously emphasized that the story of Olympic Destroyer is different to that of other threat actors because the whole attack was a masterful operation in deception.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_2",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s2"
      },
      "text": "The attackers behind Olympic Destroyer forged automatically generated signatures, known as Rich Header, to make it look like the malware was produced by Lazarus APT, an actor widely believed to be associated with North Korea.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_3",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s3"
      },
      "text": "Based on malware similarity, the Olympic Destroyer malware was linked by other researchers to three Chinese speaking APT actors and the allegedly North Korean Lazarus APT; some code had hints of the EternalRomance exploit, while other code was similar to the Netya (Expetr/NotPetya) and BadRabbit targeted ransomware.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_4",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s4"
      },
      "text": "Kaspersky Lab managed to find lateral movement tools and initial infection backdoors, and has followed the infrastructure used to control Olympic Destroyer in one of its South Korean victims.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_5",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s5"
      },
      "text": "Some of the TTPs and operational security used by Olympic Destroyer bear a certain resemblance to Sofacy APT group activity.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_19",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p3_s6"
      },
      "text": "This is a familiar technique used in other Olympic Destroyer spear-phishing documents in the past and in Powershell backdoors found in the infrastructure of Olympic Destroyer’s victims located in Pyeongchang.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_21",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s2"
      },
      "text": "Downloaded access.log.txt This file has a similar structure to the Powershell script executed by the macro in spear- phishing attachments.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_23",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s1"
      },
      "text": "Below we partially provide the http stager scriptlet for the downloaded Empire agent.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_24",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 5,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p5_s2"
      },
      "text": "Powershell Empire is a post-exploitation free and open-source framework written in Python and Powershell that allows fileless control of the compromised hosts, has modular architecture and relies on encrypted communication.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_30",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 8,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p8_s1"
      },
      "text": "Decoy document using Spiez Convergence topic Another decoy document observed in the attacks (‘Investigation_file.doc’) references the nerve agent used to poison Sergey Skripal and his daughter in Salisbury:",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_32",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 10,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p10_s1"
      },
      "text": "One of the most recent weaponized documents was uploaded to a malware scanning service from Ukraine in a file named ‘nakaz.zip’, containing ‘nakaz.doc’ (translated as ‘order.doc’ from Ukrainian).",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_35",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 12,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p12_s1"
      },
      "text": "Same MultiPage1_Layout function name used in older campaign Conclusions Despite initial expectations for it to stay low or even disappear, Olympic Destroyer has resurfaced with new attacks in Europe, Russia and Ukraine.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_40",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky_OlympicDestroyerisstillalive(06-19-2018)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 13,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p13_s5"
      },
      "text": "On the other hand, the financial targets might be another false flag operation by an actor who has already excelled at this during the Pyeongchang Olympics to redirect researchers’ attention.",
